WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:04.863
- 35,000 tourists a year
visit the Berkeley Pit.

00:00:04.863 --> 00:00:07.193
A century ago this was called
the Richest Hill on Earth.

00:00:07.193 --> 00:00:10.153
It was the Anaconda Copper Mine
in Butte, Montana.

00:00:10.153 --> 00:00:12.503
It's not rich anymore, and
it's not much of a hill.

00:00:12.503 --> 00:00:15.393
In the 1950s, the mine changed
from digging underground tunnels

00:00:15.393 --> 00:00:17.603
that followed particularly
rich veins of copper,

00:00:17.603 --> 00:00:19.523
to just excavating everything,

00:00:19.523 --> 00:00:21.313
turning the hill into an open pit

00:00:21.313 --> 00:00:24.043
on a scale that is difficult
to convey on camera.

00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:27.593
There were whole neighbourhoods destroyed
to make way for this.

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:30.273
It is a mile wide, and so
deep that you could fit

00:00:30.273 --> 00:00:34.013
the new One World Trade Center
standing upright in it.

00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:35.423
Or at least you could,

00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:39.103
if the pit wasn't halfway filled
with toxic dilute acid.

00:00:39.103 --> 00:00:41.683
It kills any birds that
land in there for too long.

00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:44.893
In 2016, thousands of geese
died in one night

00:00:44.893 --> 00:00:47.293
after a snowstorm forced them down there.

00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:51.023
The team here let me get a little bit
closer than the tourists do.

00:00:51.023 --> 00:00:52.953
- Some studies were done
to try to figure out

00:00:52.953 --> 00:00:56.323
how long a bird can withstand
sitting on the pit water

00:00:56.323 --> 00:00:59.273
and those studies found
that it's up to 18 hours.

00:00:59.273 --> 00:01:01.123
Six to seven thousand birds come through

00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:04.913
and utilise the Berkeley Pit
as a resting stop per year.

00:01:04.913 --> 00:01:07.203
We're on the overlap of two major flyways

00:01:07.203 --> 00:01:09.853
so it's to be expected.
[deep boom]

00:01:09.853 --> 00:01:11.113
- Can you tell me what that was?

00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:14.533
- So that's a propane cannon,
we have them on timers

00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:17.323
and they run 24 hours a
day, seven days a week.

00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:20.493
Hopefully, deter the birds
from ever considering

00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:23.093
landing on the water of the
Berkeley Pit to begin with.

00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:26.903
If the birds land, we
have a crew of personnel

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:29.663
that observes from this shack on the hour.

00:01:29.663 --> 00:01:32.503
They log them and then
attempt to haze them off.

00:01:32.503 --> 00:01:37.543
The go-to is high-powered rifles,
you have that ability to hit very close

00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:40.333
to the birds from a
very far distance away.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.503
And it's not so much the boom
as you would expect that scares them

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.623
but it's the projectile hitting the water,

00:01:45.623 --> 00:01:49.873
creating a large splash, and
that's what usually does it.

00:01:49.873 --> 00:01:53.263
And in the case that we know
a large flock of birds is coming,

00:01:53.263 --> 00:01:55.503
we can have continual fireworks

00:01:55.503 --> 00:01:58.703
to deter the birds from
entering the pit area.

00:01:58.703 --> 00:02:03.323
We get almost all of them,
we are over 99% effective.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:05.763
- There's around 10,000 miles
of mine shafts and tunnels

00:02:05.763 --> 00:02:07.053
under the city of Butte.

00:02:07.053 --> 00:02:09.803
That's not an exaggeration.
10,000 miles.

00:02:09.803 --> 00:02:12.543
Groundwater and rainwater had
to be constantly pumped out.

00:02:12.543 --> 00:02:16.973
Nearby pumps moved somewhere
around 300 litres a second.

00:02:16.973 --> 00:02:20.263
But when this pit shut down in 1982,
so did the pumps,

00:02:20.263 --> 00:02:22.923
and water started to flood
the old tunnels and the pit.

00:02:22.923 --> 00:02:25.003
The rock here is full of iron pyrite.

00:02:25.003 --> 00:02:29.083
Combine that with oxygen and water
and as it rusts, you get sulfuric acid.

00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:32.373
That in turn, breaks down other
metals and minerals in the rock.

00:02:32.373 --> 00:02:34.213
And the result is that
the water down there

00:02:34.213 --> 00:02:37.133
is so acidic and so
contaminated with toxins

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:40.113
that nothing except a few
hardy bugs can live in it.

00:02:40.113 --> 00:02:42.793
The deep groundwater here
is utterly contaminated

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:44.823
but it's a closed system.

00:02:44.823 --> 00:02:48.503
The sulfuric acid can't
get in or out, for now,

00:02:48.503 --> 00:02:50.473
because there's one more catch.

00:02:50.473 --> 00:02:52.453
Water is still flowing into the pit

00:02:52.453 --> 00:02:54.153
and if it gets above a certain level,

00:02:54.153 --> 00:02:56.746
then the nasty stuff
will start to leak out.

00:02:57.713 --> 00:03:01.543
- So down here we're at the
Berkeley Pit pump station.

00:03:01.543 --> 00:03:06.333
Right now the Berkeley Pit
is the sink or the sump,

00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:09.143
like a hydraulic control for the system.

00:03:09.143 --> 00:03:11.783
So what we're doing here
is we're pumping, treating

00:03:11.783 --> 00:03:13.813
and discharging water from the system.

00:03:13.813 --> 00:03:17.883
And this includes bringing
Berkeley Pit water on line

00:03:17.883 --> 00:03:21.513
and treating it through an
existing water treatment plant.

00:03:21.513 --> 00:03:25.093
If the Berkeley Pit is not maintained,

00:03:25.093 --> 00:03:29.793
the protective water level would
be reached in July of 2023.

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:35.463
pH at one time was as
low as about 2.2 or 2.3.

00:03:35.463 --> 00:03:37.823
Currently up to about 4.1.

00:03:37.823 --> 00:03:40.333
The water that we'll be
discharging from the site

00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:43.413
will be clean water, about
7 million gallons per day.

00:03:43.413 --> 00:03:45.033
2100 gallons per minute.

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:49.503
Since 1982, the water
level has been increasing,

00:03:49.503 --> 00:03:52.133
in the neighborhood of about
six to seven feet per year.

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:56.163
Later this year would be
the first time since 1982

00:03:56.163 --> 00:04:00.103
that the pit water level will
be maintained and held steady.

00:04:00.103 --> 00:04:03.773
- Tourists go here because, well,
tourists will go anywhere interesting

00:04:03.773 --> 00:04:05.523
and this is interesting.

00:04:05.523 --> 00:04:09.453
In 2007, Dr Phaedra Pezzullo of the
University of Colorado, Boulder,

00:04:09.453 --> 00:04:11.873
coined the term 'toxic tourism'.

00:04:11.873 --> 00:04:14.423
The fact that people will
come to gawp or be shocked

00:04:14.423 --> 00:04:17.643
or just to take a selfie,
and say that they saw

00:04:17.643 --> 00:04:20.573
the mile-wide pool of toxic waste.

00:04:20.573 --> 00:04:22.673
And there are much worse ways
to raise awareness

00:04:22.673 --> 00:04:25.403
and tell the world, hey,
this is pretty screwed up

00:04:25.403 --> 00:04:27.043
and we need to do something about it.

00:04:27.043 --> 00:04:30.283
A $3 admission fee is,
well, it's capitalism.

00:04:30.283 --> 00:04:35.073
In the United States, a country that codified
the idea of the roadside attraction,

00:04:35.073 --> 00:04:37.283
it almost seems normal to pay for a ticket

00:04:37.283 --> 00:04:39.996
to see the mile-wide,
incredibly toxic pit.

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:42.993
Thanks to all the team
from Montana Resources

00:04:42.993 --> 00:04:44.943
and the Berkeley Pit who
helped make this video happen.

00:04:44.943 --> 00:04:47.613
You can find out more about them
by pulling down the description.

